course: dictionary
lesson: 7902
title: A rascal can be loved
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound like insults, but they are not always meant badly. Look at who says the word and how they say it. «You little rascal!» said Gran, laughing, as the puppy chewed her slipper. Gran is not angry. Her laugh and her words show she loves the naughty puppy.
explain: A rascal is a cheeky, naughty child or animal who gets into mischief. When someone says it fondly, the loving tone is part of the meaning. Read the clues around the word: who is speaking, what happened, and how it was said.
explain: Long ago, rascal could mean something much worse. In old stories it meant a dishonest person, a rogue or a scoundrel. The same word can have more than one meaning, so the story tells you which one the writer wants.
fact: A rascal is a cheeky, naughty child or animal who makes mischief.
fact: When people say «you little rascal», they are usually being fond, not cross.
fact: In old books, «rascal» can mean a dishonest person, or a rogue.
fact: In Scotland, «ye wee rascal» is a fond telling-off, like a gentle scolding with a smile.
fact: Context clues, such as a laugh or a hug, show whether the word is kind or unkind.
exercise: Gran says, «You little rascal!» while laughing at a puppy. What does she mean? | She is describing a dishonest thief | She is very cross and wants the puppy to leave | *She is fond of the cheeky, naughty puppy | She wants to go to bed
exercise: In an old story, a man is called a rascal because he lies and steals. What does rascal mean here? | *A dishonest person, a rogue | A loved, naughty child | A small pet dog | A kind doctor
exercise: «The rascal grinned, then hugged his mum.» Which clue shows the rascal is loved? | *He hugged his mum | He was called a rascal | He grinned | His mum was tired
test: What does a rascal usually mean today when it is used about a child? | A tired, quiet child | A loved, cheeky, naughty child | A dishonest person in an old story | *A loved, cheeky, naughty child who makes mischief
test: Which clue tells you that «you little rascal» is said fondly? | The speaker is reading a book | *The speaker is laughing and smiling | The speaker is walking away | The speaker is angry and shouting
exam: Nan says «ye wee rascal» with a smile after you pinch a biscuit. What is she feeling? | Bored of the biscuits | *Fond and teasing, with a smile | Scared of you | Angry and ready to shout
